# Default config. Mount your own over /etc/tor/torrc to change anything.
#
# The hidden service directory is expected to be a mounted volume that either is empty (Tor
# mints a key and prints the new address) or already holds hs_ed25519_secret_key /
# hs_ed25519_public_key (the address you already own). Tor only ever rewrites `hostname`.
#
# Only DataDirectory is named for paths: Tor renames these options between releases (this
# config died on startup over a "StatePath", which 0.4.9 does not know), so the fewer option
# names spelled out here, the less this image breaks when the base bumps.

DataDirectory /var/lib/tor/data

Log notice stdout

# A container that does not know its cgroup limit is an OOM kill waiting to happen: tor sizes
# its queue from total system memory, which inside a pod means the node's memory (measured
# 5951 MB on a 4 GB-class node against a 384 Mi container limit). Past this threshold tor
# sheds connections; without it the kernel kills tor and the onion goes down.
MaxMemInQueues 64 MB

# Loopback SOCKS proxy: lets a sidecar (and `kubectl exec`) fetch the .onion through Tor
# itself, which is how a deployment gets verified end to end. Nothing outside the pod can
# reach it. No SocksPolicy: 0.4.9 cannot express ".onion destinations" in policy syntax
# ("Malformed IP \"*.onion\""), and a blanket "reject *" is not the way to restrict this.
SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050

HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/hidden_service
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8080
